{{!
    @template theme_dimensions/category_panel

    The panel of one area's courses, drawn between the rows of posters: a region
    labelled by its own heading, whose header names the area and how many
    courses it holds and offers its two orders and its Close; the strip of the
    area's subareas; a rail of up to eight course cards, the card template
    itself in a smaller presentation; and a footer whose call to action opens
    the area's page in the panel's order.

    One template, two renderers. The page renders it for an address carrying the
    panel, and the panel module renders it through core/templates after asking
    the listing's web service, from the same context, which the server builds.
    Without JavaScript the two orders and Close are links that reload this page
    (the orders landing on the panel, Close back on the poster); in the client's
    copy they are buttons, the orders a radio group. Only the client reaches the
    loading, skeleton and error states. Every number in a sentence arrives
    composed and formatted; every sentence carrying only the area's name is a
    string helper here, with the name as its parameter, so it renders alike on
    both sides.

    The heading is focusable (the client moves the focus there on open) and is
    described by the line under it. Each control the client may have to put the
    focus back on carries a focus key, and the four regions the client replaces
    while the header stays - the subtitle, the strip, the body and the footer -
    are always in the document, empty or hidden when they have nothing to say.

    A listed but not public area on the anonymous shell has no page a visitor
    may open, so its name is not a link, it has no strip and no call to action,
    and its footer offers the parent's own listing narrowed to the area instead.

    Under a search or a filter the sentences count results and say what they
    answer, all composed by the server. The zero state says nothing here answers
    and, when other areas of the page hold a result, suggests them: in the
    server's copy each is a link reopening this page with that area's panel, in
    the client's a button opening it in place. Both carry the same action, area
    id and focus key, so the client converts a server-drawn link into its button
    as it converts the orders and Close. The suggestions are page data, merged
    by the client like the cards. A suggestion's digit is hidden from assistive
    technology and its words ("3 results") are read in its place, so its
    accessible name never ends in a bare number.

    The rail's two arrows are a pointer convenience: hidden from assistive
    technology, out of the tab order (the focus already brings each card into
    view), and shown only with the panel module, from md up and for a pointer
    that is not coarse. The "+N" tile at the rail's end is out of the tab order
    too, because the call to action opens the same page.

    Example context (json):
